#{[17592186308593 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=4>0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable spiral (source)}.$"] [17592186308594 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) &=-x(t)-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t) &=3x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\hspace{2em}$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186308594 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-1<0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable spiral (sink)}.$"] [17592186308595 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) &=-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t) &=4x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\hspace{2em}$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186308595 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{0\\cdot t}=1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a center}.$"] [17592186308596 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) &=-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t) &=5x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =\\pm 5\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\hspace{2em}$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186308596 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{0\\cdot t}=1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a center}.$"] [17592186308597 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) &=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t) &=8x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\hspace{2em}$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186308597 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{0\\cdot t}=1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a center}.$"] [17592186308598 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) &=-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t) &=12x(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =\\pm 6\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\hspace{2em}$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186308598 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{0\\cdot t}=1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a center}.$"] [17592186310939 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186309266] [17592186311237 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186309452] [17592186311317 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186310263] [17592186311416 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186310402] [17592186311807 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=4xy+3x\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(4xy+3x),\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $x$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$"] [17592186311852 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=3y-2\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2-3y,\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $x$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$"] [17592186311855 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n3x\\hspace{0.167em}{y}^{'}=-6y-4{x}^{2}\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+6y,\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)=3x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $x$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$"] [17592186311873 :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}{y}^{'}=-x{y}^{3}-x,\\hspace{1em}x>0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form. This gives\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=x({y}^{3}+1),\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\hspace{0.278em}{{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $y$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-7}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}.\n$$"] [17592186319202 :question/worked-solution "We are given that $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\hspace{-0.167em}({\\color{red}\\frac{\\pi }{6}})={\\color{blue}1}$ into the solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{sin}(3x) \\\\ {\\color{blue}1} & =Asin\\hspace{-0.167em}(3\\cdot {\\color{red}\\frac{\\pi }{6}}) \\\\ 1 & =Asin\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ 1 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321016 :question/worked-solution "We are given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\hspace{-0.167em}({\\color{red}\\frac{\\pi }{8}})={\\color{blue}\\sqrt{2}}$ into the solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{cos}(2x) \\\\ {\\color{blue}\\sqrt{2}} & =Acos\\hspace{-0.167em}(2\\cdot {\\color{red}\\frac{\\pi }{8}}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321017 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{3x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=5.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}2})={\\color{blue}5}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{3x} \\\\ {\\color{blue}5} & =A{e}^{3\\cdot {\\color{red}2}} \\\\ 5 & =A{e}^{6} \\\\ \\frac{5}{{e}^{6}} & =A \\\\ A & =\\frac{5}{{e}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321018 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}1})={\\color{blue}2}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2} & =A{e}^{-4\\cdot {\\color{red}1}} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4} \\\\ 2{e}^{4} & =A \\\\ A & =2{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321019 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{e}^{x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1)=4.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}-1})={\\color{blue}4}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{x} \\\\ {\\color{blue}4} & =A{e}^{{\\color{red}-1}} \\\\ 4 & =A{e}^{-1} \\\\ 4e & =A \\\\ A & =4e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321021 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{4}}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}2})={\\color{blue}\\frac{3}{4}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{A}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}\\frac{3}{4}} & =\\frac{A}{{{\\color{red}2}}^{4}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{A}{16} \\\\ A & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321022 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A-x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=3.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}0})={\\color{blue}3}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{A-x} \\\\ {\\color{blue}3} & =\\frac{1}{A-{\\color{red}0}} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{A} \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186321023 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=A{x}^{3}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=4.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}2})={\\color{blue}4}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}4} & =A({\\color{red}2})^{3} \\\\ 4 & =A(8) \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323752 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}=$"] [17592186323752 :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,1,0)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,1,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(1)(0)+(0)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(1)+(-1)(0)-(1)(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0+0}{1+1+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186330301 :answer/value "-7\\operatorname{tan}x+4\\operatorname{csc}x+C"]}
